# Google Hayato (MediaTek MT8192, board codename `asurada`).
#
# One package per line; blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
# Device-agnostic packages live in packages/base.list, and the filter in
# packages/aarch64-filter.list is applied to this list too.

# Kernel: ours, from the local [platform] bundle, and the only kernel on the
# image. The release comes from the config (7.2.7-mt81); see
# kernels/mediatek-mt81 and docs/plan.md Phase 1.
linux-omarchy-book

# Firmware: MediaTek (the MT8192 SCP and its Wi-Fi/BT) and Realtek (the RTW88
# Wi-Fi and Bluetooth). Arch Linux ARM splits linux-firmware by vendor; the
# metapackage also pulls blobs for x86 GPUs this machine never loads, but it is
# kept because it carries the odd blob whose need only shows on hardware (a
# Cirrus speaker amp, say) and the bootstrap ships it anyway. The Mali-G57
# needs none: panthor's mali_csffw.bin is for Gen10, not this Bifrost part,
# which panfrost drives without firmware.
linux-firmware
linux-firmware-mediatek
linux-firmware-realtek

# ChromeOS boot tooling. vboot-utils provides cgpt, crossystem and futility;
# depthcharge-tools (built from the AUR recipe into [platform]) provides
# depthchargectl, which builds the signed FIT written to the kernel partition.
vboot-utils
depthcharge-tools

# The platform implementation the aarch64-google lifecycle resolves to
# (platform/omarchy-book and platform/omarchy-book-boot, built into
# [platform] like the kernel): the runtime half owns the keyd map, the
# brightness rule, the platform data the shell reads and the setup-system leaf;
# the boot half owns the signed kpart in p1 (setup-boot, update-verify, and the
# ALPM hook that rewrites it after a kernel update).
omarchy-book
omarchy-book-boot

# Keyboard: a Chromebook action row has no F1-F10, and keyd supplies them. The
# mapping and its exclusions ship in that platform package, not here: they are
# keyed to the `google` family rather than to this device.
keyd

# Audio. The MT8192 sound card needs its ALSA UCM BootSequence run (alsa-utils
# drives it on card add; alsa-ucm-conf carries it), or the only sink is a Dummy
# Output, and ALSA-only applications need PipeWire's default PCM.
alsa-ucm-conf
alsa-utils
pipewire-alsa

# Backlight control.
brightnessctl
